    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Overview and Motivation\n\nModern large models exceed the memory and compute capacity of a single accelerator. Two complementary parallelism strategies address this during inference:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: Splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a shard of every layer and processes the *same* tokens.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: Splits the *experts* of a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer across devices. Each device holds a few complete experts, and *different* tokens are routed to different devices.\n\nIn practice, large MoE models (e.g., DeepSeek-V3, Mixtral-class architectures, Switch-style transformers) combine both: attention and dense layers use TP, while MoE layers use EP, often with data parallelism (DP) layered on top for additional throughput.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer block with:\n\n- Attention projection: $Y = XW_Q, XW_K, XW_V$, output $Y = A W_O$\n- MLP: $Y = \\sigma(XW_1)W_2$ (or gated variants: $Y = (\\text{SiLU}(XW_g) \\odot XW_{up})W_{down}$)\n\nThe standard Megatron-style sharding for a world size $P$:\n\n| Weight | Shard dimension | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| $W_Q, W_K, W_V$ | Column (output dim) | Attention heads split cleanly across devices |\n| $W_O$ | Row (input dim) | Partial outputs sum back to full activation |\n| $W_1, W_g, W_{up}$ | Column | Intermediate activations split |\n| $W_2, W_{down}$ | Row |",
      "reasoning": "",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
